{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NDZ64XDBKAgFrk9CvtjQC5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd006-cat\n2\ttitle: \"cmd/cat: Concatenate and Display Files\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  The macOS BSD cat differs from GNU cat in flag handling (-e, -t, and -v behavior) and\n6\t  in the treatment of non-printing characters. Scripts that rely on GNU cat's -A, -v,\n7\t  -E, and -T flags for diagnostic inspection of binary or control-character content will\n8\t  behave differently on macOS. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n9\t  reference binary (gcat, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations\n10\t  including binary input, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n11\t\n12\tgoals:\n13\t  - G1: Concatenate one or more files (or stdin) to stdout with no transformation by default.\n14\t  - G2: Support line-numbering flags (-n, -b) that prepend line numbers to output.\n15\t  - G3: Support the blank-squeezing flag (-s) that collapses consecutive blank lines into one.\n16\t  - G4: Support non-printing display flags (-v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t) that make control characters and special bytes visible.\n17\t  - G5: Pass binary data through without corruption when no transformation flags are active.\n18\t\n19\trequirements:\n20\t  R1:\n21\t    title: Default behavior (no flags)\n22\t    items:\n23\t      - R1.1:\n24\t          text: Must read each named file in argument order and write its contents verbatim to stdout.\n25\t      - R1.2:\n26\t          text: Must read from stdin when no file arguments are given, or when \"-\" is given as a filename.\n27\t      - R1.3:\n28\t          text: \"Must support concatenating multiple files: each file's content is written in sequence with no separator.\"\n29\t      - R1.4:\n30\t          text: \"Must not corrupt binary input: output bytes must be identical to input bytes when no transformation flags are active.\"\n31\t      - R1.5:\n32\t          text: Must not add or remove newlines from the input.\n33\t\n34\t  R2:\n35\t    title: Line numbering (-n, -b)\n36\t    items:\n37\t      - R2.1:\n38\t          text: '-n must prepend a line number to every output line. The line number must be right-justified in a field of width 6, followed by a tab character and then the line content (format: \"%6d\\t%s\"). Line numbering resets to 1 at the start of each cat invocation, not per file.'\n39\t      - R2.2:\n40\t          text: -b must prepend a line number only to non-blank lines. Blank lines receive no number and no tab prefix. -b implies -n (activates line numbering) but overrides it by skipping blank lines.\n41\t      - R2.3:\n42\t          text: When -b and -n are both given, -b takes precedence (blank lines are not numbered). This matches cat.c:602 where number_nonblank overrides number.\n43\t      - R2.4:\n44\t          text: A blank line is a line containing only a newline character (zero non-newline bytes). Lines with spaces or tabs are not blank.\n45\t\n46\t  R3:\n47\t    title: Blank-line squeezing (-s)\n48\t    items:\n49\t      - R3.1:\n50\t          text: \"-s must suppress repeated blank lines: when two or more consecutive blank lines appear in the output, only one must be written. The first blank line is written; subsequent consecutive blank lines are discarded.\"\n51\t      - R3.2:\n52\t          text: \"-s must apply across file boundaries when multiple files are concatenated: if the last line of file A and the first line of file B are both blank, they count as consecutive blank lines.\"\n53\t      - R3.3:\n54\t          text: \"-s is compatible with -n and -b. When combined, squeezing is applied before numbering: the suppressed blank lines do not consume line numbers.\"\n55\t\n56\t  R4:\n57\t    title: Non-printing display (-v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t)\n58\t    items:\n59\t      - R4.1:\n60\t          text: '-v must display non-printing characters using caret notation and M- prefix. Control characters (0x00-0x1F except 0x09 tab and 0x0A newline) must be shown as ^X where X is the character 64 positions higher (e.g., 0x01 -> \"^A\", 0x1B -> \"^[\"). Characters in the range 0x80-0x9F must be shown as \"M-^X\". Characters in the range 0xA0-0xFE must be shown as \"M-X\" where X is the character minus 0x80. The DEL character (0x7F) must be shown as \"^?\". 0xFF must be shown as \"M-^?\".'\n61\t      - R4.2:\n62\t          text: -v must not alter tab (0x09) or newline (0x0A) characters. Those are the two control characters exempted from -v display.\n63\t      - R4.3:\n64\t          text: -E must append a \"$\" character before each newline in the output. The newline itself is still written after the \"$\".\n65\t      - R4.4:\n66\t          text: -T must display tab characters (0x09) as the two-character sequence \"^I\" rather than the tab character itself.\n67\t      - R4.5:\n68\t          text: -A is equivalent to -v -E -T combined. All three transformations are active simultaneously.\n69\t      - R4.6:\n70\t          text: -e is equivalent to -v -E combined (show non-printing, show ends).\n71\t      - R4.7:\n72\t          text: -t is equivalent to -v -T combined (show non-printing, show tabs).\n73\t      - R4.8:\n74\t          text: '-u is accepted but has no effect (cat.c:616: \"we provide the -u feature unconditionally\"). The flag must not produce an error.'\n75\t      - R4.9:\n76\t          text: \"Transformation flags are compatible with -n, -b, and -s. The order of application is: squeeze blanks (-s), then apply non-printing display (-v/-T), then append end-of-line marker (-E), then prepend line number (-n/-b).\"\n77\t\n78\t  R5:\n79\t    title: Error handling and exit codes\n80\t    items:\n81\t      - R5.1:\n82\t          text: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n83\t      - R5.2:\n84\t          text: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened. The error message must be written to stderr. cat must continue processing remaining file arguments after the error.\n85\t      - R5.3:\n86\t          text: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout (e.g., SIGPIPE or full disk). The write error must be detected and reported; the process must not hang.\n87\t      - R5.4:\n88\t          text: |\n89\t            Must not exit non-zero due to SIGPIPE when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer. At startup, install a SIGPIPE handler: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Do not use signal.Ignore (deferred cleanup functions must not run on SIGPIPE, matching GNU behavior).\n90\t\n91\tnon_goals:\n92\t  - cmd/cat does not implement the FIONREAD ioctl optimization from cat.c:298; it uses standard io.Copy which is sufficient for correctness.\n93\t  - cmd/cat does not implement POSIX unbuffered output semantics for -u; the flag is accepted but ignored.\n94\t  - cmd/cat does not implement terminal line-editing or interactive mode.\n95\t  - cmd/cat does not detect when input and output refer to the same file and warn about it (GNU cat does; this is a diagnostic, not a correctness requirement).\n96\t\n97\tacceptance_criteria:\n98\t  - id: AC1\n99\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/cat file1 file2 produces identical bytes to gcat file1 file2 for arbitrary file contents including binary.\"\n100\t    traces:\n101\t      - R1.1\n102\t      - R1.2\n103\t      - R1.3\n104\t      - R1.4\n105\t      - R1.5\n106\t  - id: AC2\n107\t    criterion: \"printf 'a\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nb\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/cat -n produces numbered lines, matching gcat -n output byte-for-byte.\"\n108\t    traces:\n109\t      - R2.1\n110\t      - R2.2\n111\t      - R2.3\n112\t      - R3.3\n113\t      - R4.9\n114\t  - id: AC3\n115\t    criterion: \"printf 'a\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nb\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/cat -s produces a single blank line between 'a' and 'b', matching gcat -s.\"\n116\t    traces:\n117\t      - R3.1\n118\t      - R3.2\n119\t      - R3.3\n120\t      - R4.9\n121\t  - id: AC4\n122\t    criterion: \"printf '\\\\x01\\\\x09\\\\x1b\\\\x7f\\\\x80\\\\xff' | go run ./cmd/cat -v produces the correct caret and M- notation, matching gcat -v byte-for-byte.\"\n123\t    traces:\n124\t      - R4.1\n125\t      - R4.2\n126\t      - R4.5\n127\t      - R4.6\n128\t      - R4.7\n129\t      - R4.9\n130\t  - id: AC5\n131\t    criterion: \"Differential test against gcat passes for all flag combinations: -n, -b, -s, -v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t, and combinations thereof, on text and binary inputs.\"\n132\t    traces:\n133\t      - R2.1\n134\t      - R2.2\n135\t      - R2.3\n136\t      - R3.1\n137\t      - R3.2\n138\t      - R3.3\n139\t      - R4.1\n140\t      - R4.2\n141\t      - R4.3\n142\t      - R4.4\n143\t      - R4.5\n144\t      - R4.6\n145\t      - R4.7\n146\t      - R4.9\n147\t  - id: AC6\n148\t    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/cat on a non-existent file prints an error to stderr, exits 1, and still processes any other files on the command line.\"\n149\t    traces:\n150\t      - R4.8\n151\t      - R5.1\n152\t      - R5.2\n153\t      - R5.3\n154\t      - R5.4\n155\t  - id: AC7\n156\t    criterion: \"cmd/cat compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n157\t    traces:\n158\t      - R1.1\n159\t      - R1.2\n160\t      - R1.3\n161\t      - R1.4\n162\t      - R1.5\n163\t      - R2.1\n164\t      - R2.2\n165\t      - R2.3\n166\t      - R2.4\n167\t      - R3.1\n168\t      - R3.2\n169\t      - R3.3\n170\t      - R4.1\n171\t      - R4.2\n172\t      - R4.3\n173\t      - R4.4\n174\t      - R4.5\n175\t      - R4.6\n176\t      - R4.7\n177\t      - R4.8\n178\t      - R4.9\n179\t      - R5.1\n180\t      - R5.2\n181\t      - R5.3\n182\t      - R5.4\n183\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a5863132-3677-423a-8171-a5253feda0d6","uuid":"77d1d7ff-6824-49a7-bc5a-f56865b72ee8","timestamp":"2026-05-14T00:52:00.204Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5111/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd006-cat.yaml","content":"id: srd006-cat\ntitle: \"cmd/cat: Concatenate and Display Files\"\n\nproblem: |\n  The macOS BSD cat differs from GNU cat in flag handling (-e, -t, and -v behavior) and\n  in the treatment of non-printing characters. Scripts that rely on GNU cat's -A, -v,\n  -E, and -T flags for diagnostic inspection of binary or control-character content will\n  behave differently on macOS. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU\n  reference binary (gcat, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations\n  including binary input, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Concatenate one or more files (or stdin) to stdout with no transformation by default.\n  - G2: Support line-numbering flags (-n, -b) that prepend line numbers to output.\n  - G3: Support the blank-squeezing flag (-s) that collapses consecutive blank lines into one.\n  - G4: Support non-printing display flags (-v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t) that make control characters and special bytes visible.\n  - G5: Pass binary data through without corruption when no transformation flags are active.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Default behavior (no flags)\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: Must read each named file in argument order and write its contents verbatim to stdout.\n      - R1.2:\n          text: Must read from stdin when no file arguments are given, or when \"-\" is given as a filename.\n      - R1.3:\n          text: \"Must support concatenating multiple files: each file's content is written in sequence with no separator.\"\n      - R1.4:\n          text: \"Must not corrupt binary input: output bytes must be identical to input bytes when no transformation flags are active.\"\n      - R1.5:\n          text: Must not add or remove newlines from the input.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Line numbering (-n, -b)\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: '-n must prepend a line number to every output line. The line number must be right-justified in a field of width 6, followed by a tab character and then the line content (format: \"%6d\\t%s\"). Line numbering resets to 1 at the start of each cat invocation, not per file.'\n      - R2.2:\n          text: -b must prepend a line number only to non-blank lines. Blank lines receive no number and no tab prefix. -b implies -n (activates line numbering) but overrides it by skipping blank lines.\n      - R2.3:\n          text: When -b and -n are both given, -b takes precedence (blank lines are not numbered). This matches cat.c:602 where number_nonblank overrides number.\n      - R2.4:\n          text: A blank line is a line containing only a newline character (zero non-newline bytes). Lines with spaces or tabs are not blank.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Blank-line squeezing (-s)\n    items:\n      - R3.1:\n          text: \"-s must suppress repeated blank lines: when two or more consecutive blank lines appear in the output, only one must be written. The first blank line is written; subsequent consecutive blank lines are discarded.\"\n      - R3.2:\n          text: \"-s must apply across file boundaries when multiple files are concatenated: if the last line of file A and the first line of file B are both blank, they count as consecutive blank lines.\"\n      - R3.3:\n          text: \"-s is compatible with -n and -b. When combined, squeezing is applied before numbering: the suppressed blank lines do not consume line numbers.\"\n\n  R4:\n    title: Non-printing display (-v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t)\n    items:\n      - R4.1:\n          text: '-v must display non-printing characters using caret notation and M- prefix. Control characters (0x00-0x1F except 0x09 tab and 0x0A newline) must be shown as ^X where X is the character 64 positions higher (e.g., 0x01 -> \"^A\", 0x1B -> \"^[\"). Characters in the range 0x80-0x9F must be shown as \"M-^X\". Characters in the range 0xA0-0xFE must be shown as \"M-X\" where X is the character minus 0x80. The DEL character (0x7F) must be shown as \"^?\". 0xFF must be shown as \"M-^?\".'\n      - R4.2:\n          text: -v must not alter tab (0x09) or newline (0x0A) characters. Those are the two control characters exempted from -v display.\n      - R4.3:\n          text: -E must append a \"$\" character before each newline in the output. The newline itself is still written after the \"$\".\n      - R4.4:\n          text: -T must display tab characters (0x09) as the two-character sequence \"^I\" rather than the tab character itself.\n      - R4.5:\n          text: -A is equivalent to -v -E -T combined. All three transformations are active simultaneously.\n      - R4.6:\n          text: -e is equivalent to -v -E combined (show non-printing, show ends).\n      - R4.7:\n          text: -t is equivalent to -v -T combined (show non-printing, show tabs).\n      - R4.8:\n          text: '-u is accepted but has no effect (cat.c:616: \"we provide the -u feature unconditionally\"). The flag must not produce an error.'\n      - R4.9:\n          text: \"Transformation flags are compatible with -n, -b, and -s. The order of application is: squeeze blanks (-s), then apply non-printing display (-v/-T), then append end-of-line marker (-E), then prepend line number (-n/-b).\"\n\n  R5:\n    title: Error handling and exit codes\n    items:\n      - R5.1:\n          text: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n      - R5.2:\n          text: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened. The error message must be written to stderr. cat must continue processing remaining file arguments after the error.\n      - R5.3:\n          text: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout (e.g., SIGPIPE or full disk). The write error must be detected and reported; the process must not hang.\n      - R5.4:\n          text: |\n            Must not exit non-zero due to SIGPIPE when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer. At startup, install a SIGPIPE handler: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Do not use signal.Ignore (deferred cleanup functions must not run on SIGPIPE, matching GNU behavior).\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/cat does not implement the FIONREAD ioctl optimization from cat.c:298; it uses standard io.Copy which is sufficient for correctness.\n  - cmd/cat does not implement POSIX unbuffered output semantics for -u; the flag is accepted but ignored.\n  - cmd/cat does not implement terminal line-editing or interactive mode.\n  - cmd/cat does not detect when input and output refer to the same file and warn about it (GNU cat does; this is a diagnostic, not a correctness requirement).\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    criterion: \"go run ./cmd/cat file1 file2 produces identical bytes to gcat file1 file2 for arbitrary file contents including binary.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R1.5\n  - id: AC2\n    criterion: \"printf 'a\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nb\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/cat -n produces numbered lines, matching gcat -n output byte-for-byte.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R3.3\n      - R4.9\n  - id: AC3\n    criterion: \"printf 'a\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nb\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/cat -s produces a single blank line between 'a' and 'b', matching gcat -s.\"\n    traces:\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n      - R4.9\n  - id: AC4\n    criterion: \"printf '\\\\x01\\\\x09\\\\x1b\\\\x7f\\\\x80\\\\xff' | go run ./cmd/cat -v produces the correct caret and M- notation, matching gcat -v byte-for-byte.\"\n    traces:\n      - R4.1\n      - R4.2\n      - R4.5\n      - R4.6\n      - R4.7\n      - R4.9\n  - id: AC5\n    criterion: \"Differential test against gcat passes for all flag combinations: -n, -b, -s, -v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t, and combinations thereof, on text and binary inputs.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n  